From 972c054989fa5d5d88accc0156ca11bb6c304ee9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: milliluk-erik <build@localhost>
Date: Mon, 10 Aug 2026 21:52:39 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 48/49] backtrace: shadow call stack, -backtrace /
 -trap-backtrace, protect faults

The 6809 has no frame pointer, so walking the S stack after a crash is
guesswork: return addresses interleave with pushed registers and locals,
and an interrupt drops a 12-byte frame wherever S points.  An emulator
does not have to guess.  One callback per instruction -- the ambient hook
-trap-history already rides -- keeps the call stack the program actually
built:

  - the instruction after a JSR/BSR/LBSR opens a frame.  Calls are
    recognised one boundary late, by the callee's first instruction, when
    the return address is already pushed and S is exact.  First opcode
    bytes only, and neither $10 nor $11 prefixes a call, so a prefixed
    opcode can never alias one.
  - a rise in the core's interrupt depth opens an interrupt frame.
  - any frame whose recorded S is below the current S has been left,
    however it was left.  RTS, RTI, `puls pc`, LEAS unwinding and
    longjmp-style tricks all fall out of this one rule; the return path
    needs no opcode knowledge at all.

-trap-backtrace dumps it, symbolized, when a trap fires -- `-trap
xpc=@DrawBrush -trap-backtrace` answers "who calls this" without a
debugger attached.  A protect fault dumps it on stderr next to the
register report.  -backtrace arms it alone; protect mode or any
-trap-backtrace action implies it; -no-backtrace refuses both
implications (the -headless rule).

The cores grow get_sp / get_irq_depth delegates beside get_pc, restored
for the same reason get_pc was: the machine's debug_cpu copy is taken
before coco3_finish wires the emuext delegates, so anything wired at CPU
creation is the only thing reliably in it.

The opcode peek reads FLAT PHYSICAL RAM (flat_ram_peek), not the
machine's debug bus read.  The first attempt used machine->read_byte and
promptly faulted -protect-mode-read on the program's own code: the debug
read routes through the same memory path the protect checks watch, and
arrived classified with the previous cycle's stale access class.  The
flat peek touches no machine state.  Its one lie is a PC executing from
ROM, where the MMU translation is fiction and the peek reads whatever RAM
sits behind it: boot-time frames may be junk, frames after the program
takes over are exact, and the S rule keeps the stack consistent either
way.

Known limits, deliberate: a program running multiple stacks (a task
switcher repointing S) interleaves the tasks' frames, and a call whose
target is preempted by an interrupt before its first instruction loses
that one frame.  Both degrade the dump, never the emulation.

The flat-RAM lookup goes through flat_ram_resolve (patch 9), the same
resolution every consumer of flat physical RAM uses (-load-ram, the gdb
stub): one part "RAM" everywhere but the MC-10, whose RAM0+RAM1 pair
spans as a single flat space, expansion included.  The pair is resolved
once, at attach; the per-instruction peek stays a compare and the same
arithmetic as before, never a part lookup.  On the MC-10 itself the
6803's missing stack-pointer delegate still refuses the backtrace first
-- loudly, as before -- but the RAM lookup is no longer a second, silent
way to fail.
